# Prospective native pack-policy adapter

This isolated copy tests the already frozen multi-data-blob candidate through the exact production host, catalog and storage modules on private ephemeral ports and owned temporary data. Production and `work/restic-pack-aggregation-next` are not modified. The original 66-test candidate receipt remains historical and unchanged.

`baseline.json` identifies every copied source. `baseline/` retains exact original bytes. The old `native.test.mjs` is copied from the frozen stage, including its original assertions. `adapter-diff.json` records every additional import/assertion/fixture transformation. Each `runs/` directory contains raw output, exact pre-run sources, before/after fingerprints and result counts. Oracle binaries remain local under `projects/`, excluded from the evidence manifest.

## Historical failures and subsequent adjustments

The initial unadapted native suite passed 27 of 30 tests and failed three. Two failures were the old `newPacks >= 7` policy assertion. That minimum already failed in the root's current unbatched Rabin production run according to its separately retained suite; it is not attributed here to a batching algorithm failure. This stage replaces the minimum with authenticated physical pack hashes, sealed header rows, actual payload authentication/decompression/plaintext hashes, index offsets/lengths, snapshot content ID accounting, and exact repeated-snapshot data-pack/index-row deduplication. The batch-specific assertion additionally requires the owned three-file fixture to occupy one data pack within 16 MiB. All original check/restore/timestamp/overwrite/lock assertions remain present.

The third initial failure was the mutation fixture's publication timing assumption: its 4 MiB+1 repeated-byte file reached EOF and final source checks before the first pending aggregate pack was published. Appending after that observed publication was therefore too late to exercise mutation during file capture. It is not evidence that an already completed source capture must track future filesystem changes. The initial source and raw failure remain unchanged. A separately recorded post-failure fixture adjustment uses 24 MiB+1 deterministic xorshift32 bytes (seed `0x1a2b3c4d`, low byte per state), SHA-256 `30ef6132bb13a7945b1f5c5d8155ccc607a315f1fc5272f7f560b3bac491c366`. Distinct chunks cross the 16 MiB pack target before EOF. Every existing mutation error, no-snapshot, own-lock release and allowed immutable orphan-pack assertion is retained.

Two new deterministic tiny-file probes independently avoid the publication-timing assumption. A real first source read is held in a child test, then the owned named file is appended; unchanged production capture rejects before publishing data/index/snapshot objects and closes its source handle. A real EOF chunk's owned test sink is held before completion, then its source is appended; the unchanged capture primitive rejects after the sink, closes its handle and proves emitted bytes do not alias the changed source. The latter is a primitive test with an owned sink, not a repository snapshot or original-tool comparison.

The interim policy/host-focused run passed four substantive tests. The later mutation-focused runner reports four passes: three substantive mutation tests plus Node's empty filtered host-file harness pass. Those are explicitly distinct counts, not four mutation comparisons. The final complete adapter run includes the 30 existing native tests, one new private HTTP host test, and two new held-boundary tests: 33 substantive cases.

## Original interoperability and host scope

Existing native v1/v2 original-created and app-created exchange, malformed timestamp and lock checks execute unchanged after the assertion replacement. The original restic 0.19.1 release ZIP SHA-256 is `da948ad707ed690426473aaba2046cd61f8f90f6f0e7dab6be0d5796531de67d`; its Windows amd64 executable SHA-256 is `b0dd1fd21eea5d8fe1325f55f7118213c21f36de8a261e04c0624a5ab9fd7830`. Their pinned original source and BSD-2-Clause provenance are bound by the main frozen stage. No original application is used as the candidate runtime.

The added private host test captures exactly three regular files (binary, Unicode text and empty file), verifies one authenticated aggregate data pack and the exact snapshot content IDs, repeats with zero new data packs/rows, restarts the private host, checks persistence and restores byte-for-byte through HTTP with millisecond mtime and empty-directory checks. It then authenticates and invokes the original executable for two separately recorded commands: `check --read-data` and restore, with digest comparison. Existing user/shared hosts are untouched. There is no full-product, security or timed-performance parity claim.

The root test adapter uses exact `native-policy-assertions.mjs` SHA-256 `2cad61b73ec5542f16e7ce01ac76a80864b78ef64750018262ea36975a8e7169`, retained in `root-adapter-baseline/native-policy-assertions.mjs.snapshot`. It uses only generic `verifyStructure` and `verifyUnchanged`, which support current single-blob packs as well as candidate multi-blob packs. This prospective candidate suite also uses `verifyBatch`; that stricter policy is not imposed on current production.

This evidence is finite source-stable correctness/interoperability coverage. Filesystem capture is not an atomic snapshot. Deadline guards remain cooperative and cannot preempt blocked I/O or synchronous crypto/compression. Existing 64 MiB per-file, 16 MiB metadata, aggregate resource and cleanup limits remain as documented in the unchanged main stage.
